{"id":"winners-connections","title":"Are the persistent winners connected?","question":"For the payees named in the screens above, is there campaign giving to those who approve their files, lobbying of those officials, gifts to the paying department’s staff, or a budget add-back?","finding":"108 payees of $1 million or more are named in the rising-share, never-competed, amended-again, one-contract, faster-at-the-Board or repeated-exception screens. For 82 of them none of the four ties appears. For 21, the organization or people listing it as their employer gave $1,000 or more to committees of candidates for supervisor or mayor since 2017 (7 gave to a supervisor who sponsored one of the payee’s own files); the giving by all 108 adds up to $71,831, of which $0 came from an organization itself and the rest from people who list it as employer; 7 had lobbyists report contacts with supervisors or the Mayor’s office; 7 appear in gift disclosures by staff of a department that pays them; 0 are named in a budget add-back. 8 show two or more kinds of tie: Pacific Gas & Electric Co (an exception every time); San Francisco Aids Foundation (amended again and again; an exception every time); Healthright 360 (an exception every time); Safeway Inc (never competed).","how":"Payees are those flagged by the other screens in this file. Campaign giving is itemized contributions (in money or in kind) from 2017 to 2026 to committees of candidates for supervisor or mayor, where the contributor is the organization or a person who lists it as employer; the employer or contributor name must equal the payee’s name after standard clean-up, so this is a floor. \"To sponsors of its own files\" counts gifts to a supervisor who is listed as sponsor of a Board file naming the payee. Lobbying is contacts with officials of the Board of Supervisors or the Mayor’s office reported by registered lobbyists who name the payee as client. Gifts are disclosures by officials in a department that pays the payee (the “gifts vs payments” results). Add-backs are the budget lines that name an organization (the site’s “Earmarks, bids and other public bodies” page). Ranked by number of kinds of tie.","limits":"People give on their own account: an employee’s gift is not the employer’s. A large employer will have staff who give to someone. Employer and client names are free text and are matched only when they equal the payee’s name, so some ties are missed and a shared name is possible. A tie beside a persistent pattern is a reason to read the file, not a finding. What would settle a row: the dates of the gifts and contacts against the dates the payee’s files were introduced and voted, and the recusal record of the official concerned. The paid column counts payments issued in fiscal 2019 to 2026, which is read from the voucher number and not from the year the file lists a line under; $992.6 million of outside spending that the file lists under those years was issued earlier and is left out. Amounts, parties and dates taken from Board file titles are read by machine: in a hand check of 50 titles, 11 had at least one field read wrongly (4 after later fixes).","tier":"REDFLAG","columns":[{"k":"payee","t":"Payee","type":"org"},{"k":"why","t":"Listed above for","type":"text"},{"k":"paid","t":"Paid, fiscal 2019–2026","type":"usd"},{"k":"department","t":"Main department","type":"text"},{"k":"gave","t":"Given to supervisor and mayoral campaigns (organization and staff)","type":"usd"},{"k":"ownSponsors","t":"Of which to sponsors of its own files","type":"usd"},{"k":"mostTo","t":"Most to","type":"text"},{"k":"lobbying","t":"Lobbying contacts with supervisors or the Mayor’s office","type":"int"},{"k":"giftsToStaff","t":"Gift disclosures by paying department’s staff","type":"int"},{"k":"signs","t":"Warning signs elsewhere","type":"int"}],"rows":[{"why":"an exception every time","paid":350892258,"department":"Public Utilities Commission","gave":11733,"gaveOrg":null,"ownSponsors":750,"mostTo":"L